@*
    The Backups plugin's view: the area frame, the sub-surface strip, and the
    dialogs. Every region of the surface is its own component, and every piece
    of state and every operation lives in this component's partials, so no file
    in the plugin carries more than one concern.

    The panel takes exactly one cluster-facing service from the host - the
    per-plugin host context factory - and resolves its declared IBackupsDomain
    from the context it returns, so its reach into the cluster is what
    BackupsAreaPlugin declares and nothing more (epic decision D3). The two
    shell-state services it also injects reach nothing in a cluster: they carry
    where the caller is and where they were last time.
*@

<section class="lx-backups" aria-label="Backups">
    <header class="lx-backups-header">
        <button type="button" class="lx-btn" disabled="@Busy" @onclick="ReloadAsync">Refresh</button>
        <LatticeHelp Id="@BackupsVocabulary.ScopeStatusId"
                     Term="@BackupsVocabulary.ScopeStatus.Label"
                     Explanation="@BackupsVocabulary.ScopeStatus.Explanation" />
    </header>

    @if (LastResult is { } result)
    {
        <p class="lx-backups-result @ResultClass(result.Status)" role="status">@result.Message</p>
    }

    @*
        The area's own sub-surfaces, rendered through the design system's single
        tab primitive in its subordinate variant. That is the presentation the
        shell reserves for a plugin's own surfaces, so this strip reads as
        sitting below the rail that opened the area rather than as a fourth peer
        strip - and the primitive supplies the roving tabindex, the arrow keys,
        the enumerated aria-selected and the aria-controls relationship that the
        hand-rolled strip it replaces had to restate for itself.
    *@
    <LatticeAdaptiveTabs Tabs="BackupsSurfaces.Tabs"
                         ActiveId="@ActiveSurfaceId"
                         OnSelect="SelectSurface"
                         Label="@BackupsSurfaces.StripLabel"
                         Variant="LatticeTabsVariant.Subordinate"
                         Id="@BackupsSurfaces.StripElementId"
                         PanelId="@BackupsSurfaces.PanelElementId"
                         Class="lx-backups-surfaces" />

    <div class="lx-backups-surface"
         role="tabpanel"
         id="@BackupsSurfaces.PanelElementId"
         aria-busy="@(SurfaceRestored ? "false" : "true")">
        @if (!SurfaceRestored)
        {
            @*
                The retained surface has not resolved yet, so neither body is the
                one to render. Saying so beats rendering an empty panel: a caller
                who never gets past this - browser storage unreachable, script
                disabled - is looking at a real state with a next step rather
                than at a blank area.
            *@
            <BackupsStateBlock Message="RestoringMessage" />
        }
        else if (ActiveSubTab == BackupsSubTab.New)
        {
            <BackupsCaptureForm Owner="this" />
        }
        else
        {
            <BackupsCatalogue Owner="this" />
        }
    </div>

    <BackupsDeleteDialog Owner="this" />
    <BackupsRestoreDialog Owner="this" />
    <BackupsScheduleDialog Owner="this" />
    <BackupsHealthDialog Owner="this" />
</section>
